  "solutions": [
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function removeDuplicates(nums) {\n  const arr = nums.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const res = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n    if (i === 0 || arr[i] !== arr[i - 1]) {\n      res.push(arr[i]);\n    }\n  }\n  return res;\n}",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(1)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function removeDuplicates(nums) {\n  if (nums.length === 0) return [];\n  const arr = nums.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  let writeIdx = 1;\n  for (let readIdx = 1; readIdx < arr.length; readIdx++) {\n    if (arr[readIdx] !== arr[readIdx - 1]) {\n      arr[writeIdx++] = arr[readIdx];\n    }\n  }\n  return arr.slice(0, writeIdx);\n}",
      "isReference": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def removeDuplicates(nums):\n    arr = sorted(nums[:])\n    res = []\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        if i == 0 or arr[i] != arr[i - 1]:\n            res.append(arr[i])\n    return res\n",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(1)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def removeDuplicates(nums):\n    if len(nums) == 0:\n        return []\n    arr = sorted(nums[:])\n    write_idx = 1\n    for read_idx in range(1, len(arr)):\n        if arr[read_idx] != arr[read_idx - 1]:\n            arr[write_idx] = arr[read_idx]\n            write_idx += 1\n    return arr[:write_idx]\n",
      "isReference": true
    }
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute-force approach sorts the array and builds a new list by skipping consecutive duplicates, yielding O(n log n) time and O(n) space. The optimal approach applies the same deduplication in-place with a write pointer that only advances when the current value differs from the previous one, achieving O(1) extra space.",
    "why_optimal": "Both approaches sort first (O(n log n)), but the optimal avoids allocating a second array. On inputs where many duplicates collapse to few unique values (e.g., all-100 arrays), the write pointer barely moves, making the dedup pass nearly a single scan after sorting.",
    "pitfalls": "Not sorting first means duplicates are not adjacent and won't be caught. Confusing the write pointer with the read pointer causes elements to be overwritten prematurely. Returning the full truncated array instead of just the unique elements slice causes mismatches."
  },
  "reading_links": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-place_algorithm"
  ]
}
